@meta
id: 09-12
title: Yank takes a motion, and p puts it back
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 34
minutes: 5
vscode: select the code, Ctrl-C, click, Ctrl-V — twice
open: errors.json

@brief
Every error in this file needs its `message` filled in, and for now the
message is just the code itself spelled out again.

Fill both empty message strings so that `"message": "ORDER_NOT_FOUND"`
and `"message": "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED"` read back the code on their own
line. Nothing else about the file changes.

Copy the codes rather than retyping them. A typo here is a 500 that
nobody notices for a week.

@keys
| `yw` | yank from the cursor to the start of the next word |
| `p` | put what you yanked after the cursor |
| `P` | put it before the cursor |
| `2F"` | back to the second double quote behind you |

@hints
`y` is an operator like the others, so it takes a motion and copies
exactly the ground that motion covers. The quote characters are not part
of the code, so do not include them.
--
With the cursor on the first letter of the code, `yw` copies the whole
identifier — underscores are word characters, and the closing quote stops
it. Then get inside the empty pair of quotes and `p`.
--
Per line: `f"` then `l` to sit on the first letter, `yw`, then `$` and
`2F"` to land on the opening quote of the empty string, then `p`.

@solution
    j f" l yw $ 2F" p    j 0 f" l yw $ 2F" p    :wq<Enter>

Where did the text go? Into the unnamed register, which is the same place
`dd`, `x` and `c` put what they take. `p` reads it back. That is why
deleting a line and putting it somewhere else works as a move — there is
one clipboard and every destructive operator writes to it. Chapter 20
gives you twenty-six more of them.

`p` after a charwise yank puts the text *after* the cursor, which is why
landing on the opening quote and pressing `p` drops the code neatly
between the quotes. `P` puts it before the cursor. After a linewise yank
like `yy`, the same two keys put whole lines below or above instead. The
operator decided which kind of copy it was; `p` just respects it.

The general habit: if text already exists in the file, yank it. Retyping
an identifier is how `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPED` gets into production.

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false, "message": "" },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false, "message": "" }
}

@expected errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false, "message": "ORDER_NOT_FOUND" },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false, "message": "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED" }
}
